      // [src_shepard_spacereview, src_shepard_newsweek, src_shepard_cnn_obit, src_shepard_shepard_oh | 1959-1961 | biography review + colleague's remembrance + obituary + his own interview] The Space Review says Thompson's biography repeatedly shows him as an excellent pilot and an intense competitor, particularly when he and John Glenn jockeyed for the first Mercury flight; a fellow Mercury astronaut writes in Newsweek that he was determined to be head of the pack, desperately wanted to be first in space and reacted to Gagarin's flight with language that could not be printed; CNN's obituary, drawing on Wolfe, calls him deadly earnest about being an astronaut and quotes his later description of being chosen first of the seven as 'competition at its best'; in his own 1998 interview he recalls thinking that his peers had better be happy because he was going to make the first flight. Four voices converge on a drive central to how he is remembered, mostly as characterisations with a few concrete reactions, and one is self-reported, so documented at a moderate-high confidence and a score below the top band; dual-edged because the same drive shows up in accounts of friction and aloofness.
