      // [src_ive_cooperhewitt, src_ive_cambridge, src_ive_designmuseum, src_ive_newyorker, src_ive_wired, src_ive_tidbits, src_ive_cnbc | 2007-2019 | award body + university + museum + long-form profile + technology magazine + critical commentary] The Cooper Hewitt says he designed sculptural, desirable objects, Cambridge says he is credited with bringing elegance, purity and beauty to personal-computer design, and the Design Museum says the iMac brought colour and light to computing; Parker reports him objecting that a proposed orange-brown box lining looked like carpeting in a dismal student flat, describing tangency breaks and Bezier surfaces in the corners of products and app icons, MoMA's design curator calling the breathing sleep light just amazing, and a British designer who has known him for years calling his approach emotionally warm modernism; Wired reports that he rejected Watch sounds as too metallic or not organic enough. Dual-edged because TidBITS argues that a 'fetish for thinness' under Ive led to unpopular trade-offs such as the butterfly keyboard and lists flops from his tenure, and CNBC says his products were gorgeous but, at least with the older MacBook keyboard, sometimes at the cost of function, while calling its explanation for Apple's later shift a theory. The sensitivity itself is documented by several independent voices, but the praise is largely institutional and the criticism is commentary rather than reporting, so documented at a moderate-high confidence. LEAD REVIEW CORRECTION (Roster50, before eligibility, downward only): the behavioural account rests heavily on two access-granted long-form profiles (The New Yorker, Wired) written inside the studio, with institutional pages that are admiring; score and confidence lowered.
      aesthetic_sensitivity: [80, 0.62, "d", "D"],
      // [src_ive_wired, src_ive_newyorker, src_ive_metropolis | 2006-2015 | technology magazine + long-form profile + design magazine] Wired reports that in weekly reviews of the Watch's sounds and Taptic taps he rejected versions as too metallic or not organic enough and that reaching sounds he was happy with took more than a year; Parker reports that it took a year before he settled on the Watch's click-in straps, quotes him on the campus visitor-centre design ('we should be done, but we're still redoing and redoing') and quotes him that everything the studio makes could be described as partially wrong because it is not perfect, which he treats as a reason to try to fix it next time; Metropolis (2006) calls the studio's translation of ideas into products a process of obsessive perfectionism and quotes his regret that early versions of the iMac G5 were dominated by the camera. Three independent outlets over nine years agree, but the standard is mostly shown through his own words and colleagues' accounts in admiring features, so documented at a moderate-high confidence. LEAD REVIEW CORRECTION (Roster50, before eligibility, downward only): the behavioural account rests heavily on two access-granted long-form profiles (The New Yorker, Wired) written inside the studio, with institutional pages that are admiring; score and confidence lowered.
