      creative_originality: [74, 0.58, "s", "A"],
      // [src_ive_metropolis, src_ive_newyorker, src_ive_wired, src_ive_icon | 2003-2015 | design magazine + long-form profile + technology magazine + design magazine] Metropolis (2006) reports that at Apple design, engineering and software development proceed in tandem and that he is as concerned with a product's internal architecture as with its exterior; Parker quotes Bob Mansfield that by the iPhone he was involved in fundamentals such as how to build products efficiently, the technology and how to cool them, and reports that he and Dye decided to avoid the screen edge to blur the line between hardware and software, with the Watch typeface echoing the case's rounded squareness; Wired says iOS 7 marked his ascendance over all Apple design; Icon (2003) quotes him attributing the iPod's success to the integration of hardware, software and physical interface. The pattern is consistent across outlets, but several statements are his own or describe a team decision, so strong inference at a moderate confidence.
