      // [src_ive_newyorker, src_ive_guardian, src_ive_dezeen_kahney, src_ive_britannica | 1992-2019 | long-form profile + newspaper profile + biographer interview + encyclopaedia] The Guardian (2007) says that when he joined Apple in 1992 the company was in disarray and he at first found the work frustrating, and Kahney says he struggled for five years to get his designs made before Jobs returned; Parker reports that in the mid-1990s he was close to leaving (Clive Grinyer), that Robert Brunner recommended him as his successor and later tried to tempt him to Pentagram, that Ive told Brunner he had to wait it out, and that, assuming the worst, he had a resignation letter in his pocket when Jobs returned; Britannica dates his Apple career to 1992-2019. Several independent accounts of staying through a lean period and a long tenure, but tenure alone is weak evidence and the letter detail is reported without a named source. Unnamed-source reports from 2019 that he was less involved after 2015 were disputed by Apple's chief executive and are not used; they could temper this row for 2015-2019, which the evidence above does not cover. So strong inference at a moderate confidence.
