      // [src_levimontalcini_guardian, src_levimontalcini_pnas, src_levimontalcini_nature_blog, src_levimontalcini_accademia_xl | 1950s-2012 | press obituary + science-writer profile with colleagues' comments + journal blog obituary + academy note by a colleague] The Guardian reports Cohen's remark that she worked at great intensity during her years at Washington University and says she kept a strict and rigorous work schedule to the end of her life, with a last paper in 2012; PNAS has an EBRI postdoctoral colleague saying she came to the institute's laboratory almost every day in the late 2000s, and Cattaneo describes her daily visits to discuss data; Nature's obituary blog speaks of an obsessive commitment to the topic, and Calissano, who saw her daily for more than four decades, describes a near-monastic dedication to work. Four observers across different decades describe regular, intense work habits, but these are brief characterisations by admiring colleagues and journalists rather than a measured routine, and the evidence overlaps with the persistence row (which concerns continuing through setbacks and the NGF programme, not the daily schedule), so strong inference at a modest confidence.
